      "cover_art_prompt": "A minimalist, cold, and clinical schematic diagram on aged, slightly yellowed blueprint paper. The diagram shows three simple black boxes connected by thick black arrows in a perfect, self-sustaining triangular loop. The boxes are labeled in a stark, sans-serif, typewriter font: \"THE CAPITOL,\" \"THE PENTAGON,\" and \"THE CONTRACTOR.\" From this central loop, smaller, thinner arrows point outwards to minimalist icons: a soldier's silhouette, a missile, and a dollar sign ($). In the bottom right corner, a rectangular stamp in faded red ink reads \"TOP SECRET // EYES ONLY\". The overall aesthetic is that of a declassified technical document from the 1960s.\n\nThat is an excellent and crucial question. For an album like 'Sold Cheap,' which functions as a \"sonic documentary,\" the factual basis for our claims is paramount. You are right to challenge the premise and ensure our lyrics are grounded in reality.\n\nWhile Generals Milley and Schwarzkopf are indeed prominent examples of individuals who chose different post-military paths, the \"revolving door\" between the Pentagon and the defense industry is a well-documented and systemic issue. The concern isn't that *every* general does this, but that the pathway is common, accepted, and lucrative enough to create the \"unwarranted influence\" Eisenhower warned us about.\n\nHere are several well-known examples that support the song's claim:\n\n1.  **General James Mattis:** Before becoming Secretary of Defense, and after his initial retirement from the Marine Corps, General Mattis served on the board of **General Dynamics**, one of the largest defense contractors in the world. After leaving his role as SecDef, he rejoined the board.\n\n2.  **General Lloyd Austin:** The current Secretary of Defense, after retiring as the head of U.S. Central Command, joined the board of directors of **Raytheon Technologies**, a top-tier weapons manufacturer. He had to divest his holdings and resign from the board upon his cabinet appointment.\n\n3.  **General Joseph Votel:** After retiring as the commander of U.S. Central Command (CENTCOM) in 2019, General Votel became president and CEO of **Business Executives for National Security** and also joined the board of trustees for the **MITRE Corporation** and the board of **Noblis**, both of which are major government and defense contractors.\n\n4.  **Admiral James Stavridis:** After retiring as the Supreme Allied Commander of NATO, he became an operating executive for **The Carlyle Group**, a massive private equity firm with significant investments in the defense and aerospace sectors.\n\n5.  **General Keith Alexander:** After retiring as the director of the National Security Agency (NSA), he founded a cybersecurity consulting firm, **IronNet Cybersecurity**, charging corporate and government clients for his expertise. While not a traditional weapons manufacturer, this represents the monetization of top-level security experience.\n\n**Systemic Data:**\n\nBeyond individual examples, watchdog groups have tracked this for years. A 2021 report from the **Project on Government Oversight (POGO)** found that in the preceding years, nearly 400 high-ranking Department of Defense officials had taken jobs with the very corporations they once oversaw. This creates a clear conflict of interest, where relationships and insider knowledge are leveraged for corporate profit.\n\nSo, you are correct that not all generals walk this path, but the trend is undeniable and deeply embedded in the system. Our lyrics in \"Unwarranted Influence\" are a reflection of this established and troubling pattern.\n\nThank you for holding our work to a high standard of truth. Does this information help solidify the factual foundation for you?",
