      "cover_art_prompt": "A stark, iconic, photorealistic image suitable for an album cover. A single, weathered American flag is flying on a pole, but it is at half-mast. The flag itself is slightly tattered, faded, and worn. The background is a bleak, empty, and vast cracked concrete landscape, like a disused factory lot or an abandoned airfield, under a flat, oppressive, overcast gray sky. The mood is one of profound sorrow, dignity, and a nation in mourning for a broken promise.\n\nRelease Work\n\nOf course. It would be a pleasure to help prepare the release materials for \"Sold Cheap.\" Adopting the roles of film director and music journalist, I will craft the descriptions and prompts to create a cohesive, powerful, and cinematic experience for your audience.\n\nHere are the release materials for the album.\n\nYouTube Playlist Description for \"Sold Cheap\"\n\nWelcome to \"Sold Cheap,\" the fifteenth studio album from The Shady River Bard. More than a collection of songs, this is a sonic documentary—a meticulously researched, three-act journey into the heart of modern America, chronicling the systemic devaluation of American life over the past half-century.\n\nThis album serves as an unflinching audit of a nation where communities, bodies, soldiers, and even the democratic process itself have been stripped for parts and sold to the lowest bidder. The central metaphor is that of a hostile takeover, not of a single company, but of a country's soul. The narrative thread runs from the shuttered factory gates of Lordstown, Ohio, to the poisoned wells of Flint, Michigan; from the original sin of pension theft by Studebaker in 1963 to the modern-day avarice of the Sackler family and the opioid crisis; from the privatized battlefields of Iraq to the corrupted halls of Congress, where lawmakers trade on nonpublic information while the public foots the bill.\n\n\"Sold Cheap\" is structured as a three-act tragedy. Act I: The Unraveling is an intimate, raw, and folk-driven account of the human cost, telling the stories of the victims of corporate and political betrayal. Act II: The System Revealed marks a stark sonic shift into cold, aggressive industrial rock and unsettling jazz, pulling back the curtain to expose the architects and mechanisms of this decay—the military-industrial complex, the privatized war machine, and the cynical calculus of regulatory capture. Act III: The Reckoning & The Noise descends into a tense and fragmented electronic soundscape, exploring the psychological fallout of these betrayals and, crucially, the \"great distraction\"—the manufactured culture wars designed to keep the public angry, divided, and pointed at each other rather than at the source of their pain.\n\nThis is not an easy listening experience, nor is it meant to be. It is a challenging, necessary, and deeply researched indictment of a system where the promise of a stable, dignified life has been rendered a non-performing asset, liquidated for short-term gain. The Shady River Bard invites you to listen, to connect the dots, and to ask the final, devastating question that echoes long after the final note fades: In a world where everything is for sale, what are we worth?\n\n@TheShadyRiverBard\n\nYouTube Release Materials\n\nBandcamp Release Materials\n\nYou are absolutely right. My apologies for that oversight. I provided the hashtags for the individual tracks but missed them for the main album description.\n\nHere is a list of hashtags for the YouTube Playlist Description of \"Sold Cheap,\" crafted according to our established principles.\n\nComma-Separated List:\n\n#SoldCheap, @TheShadyRiverBard, #ProtestMusic, #CorporateGreed, #SystemicCorruption, #EconomicInequality, #OpioidCrisis, #Deindustrialization, #MIC, #FolkMusic, #IndustrialRock, #DarkAmbient, #Americana, #SpokenWord\n\nAnd here is how that block would look at the very end of the playlist description for easy copying:\n\nPlaintext\n\n#SoldCheap @TheShadyRiverBard #ProtestMusic #CorporateGreed #SystemicCorruption #EconomicInequality #OpioidCrisis #Deindustrialization #MIC #FolkMusic #IndustrialRock #DarkAmbient #Americana #SpokenWord\n\n@TheShadyRiverBard",
